# Coding standards — Cuidado em Par

## Language split (non-negotiable)
- **Code and database identifiers:** English only (files, folders, functions, variables, types, SQL tables/columns, constraints, enums, tag **slugs**, commits).
- **User-facing copy:** Brazilian Portuguese (`pt-BR`), inclusive and neutral (UX Writer). Includes `tags.label`, UI strings, emails, errors shown on screen.
- **Agent replies to the product team:** Portuguese, as today.
- Comments in code: English, short, only when the why is non-obvious.

## Naming
- TypeScript/JavaScript: `camelCase` for values, `PascalCase` for components/types, `UPPER_SNAKE` for true constants.
- SQL: `snake_case` for tables, columns, functions, indexes.
- Files/folders: `kebab-case` (e.g. `care-circle`, `habit-prefs`).
- Boolean names: `is_`, `has_`, `can_` prefixes (`isPublished`, `hasAcceptedConsent`).
- Tag slugs: English kebab-case (`torn-acl`, `pregnancy-trimester-3`). Never rename a published slug without a migration and Educador/Nutri review.
- Do not mix languages in one identifier (`userPerfil`, `listaExercises`).

## Database values stored in code
Stored enum-like values are English, for example:
- `health_focus`: `quality-of-life` | `physical-preparation` | `maintenance` | `body-composition`
- `diet_pattern`: `no-restriction` | `vegetarian` | `vegan`
- `cycle mode`: `menstrual-cycle` | `pregnancy` | `postpartum`
- habit / care event `kind`: `water` | `active-pause` | `workout` | `meal`
- `meal_slot`: `breakfast` | `lunch` | `snack` | `dinner`

Map to pt-BR only at the UI boundary (labels, `tags.label`).

## Stack conventions
- Next.js App Router: Server Components by default; Client Components only for real interactivity.
- Supabase: validate `auth.uid()` on the server; never expose `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` to the client.
- Prefer named exports for components and pure functions.

## Who enforces
Arquiteto, Backend, Frontend, DevOps and QA follow this rule on every change. UX Writer owns visible pt-BR strings, not code identifiers.
